    "result_text": "The specification is already fully satisfied by the current code, and the full test suite is green. Here's what I verified:\n\n**Rejection handling** \u2014 Every 400/409/429 rejection in the app flows through `ExceptionControllerAdvice` (`src/main/java/.../rest/advice/ExceptionControllerAdvice.java`):\n\n| Status | Exception handler |\n|--------|-------------------|\n| 400 | `MethodArgumentNotValidException`, `InvalidOwnerFieldsException`, `FutureRegistrationDateException` |\n| 409 | `DuplicateOwnerIdentityException`, `OwnerCityCapacityExceededException` |\n| 429 | `DailyOwnerLimitExceededException` |\n\nEach builds its body via `detailBuild(...)`, which returns a Spring `ProblemDetail` populated with all four required RFC7807 members:\n- `type` (`setType`), `title` (`setTitle`), `status` (`ProblemDetail.forStatus`), `detail` (`setDetail`).\n\nReturning `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>` makes Spring serialize the body as `application/problem+json`, and the HTTP status codes are unchanged.\n\n**No bypass paths** \u2014 I searched the whole main tree: there are no other `@ControllerAdvice`/`@RestControllerAdvice`, no `@ResponseStatus`/`ResponseStatusException`, and no controller returning a 400/409/429 body directly. All such rejections funnel through the advice.\n\n**Tests** \u2014 Ran the full suite (`./mvnw -o test`):\n```\nTests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0, Skipped: 0\nBUILD SUCCESS\n```\nThe new `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectionIsProblemJson` (asserts a rejected owner create returns content type `application/problem+json` with `$.status == 400`) passes, along with the rest of the suite.\n\nNo code changes were needed \u2014 the ProblemDetail-based advice already implements the required behavior, and the suite is green.",
